Continuing on (yes, I'll be posting the entire thing on a webpage once
it's done and putting a link up here).  This installment covers the two
conduct-enforcing classes, which I've never had much luck at all with;
these are the two I'm most concerned about in terms of giving accurate
information, so please, please, please correct me if I'm wrong (or if
any of my advice is sub-optimal).  Yes, I'll be applying anything I
learn to my own games.  :-)

-=-=-=-
Knights:
Knights can be a challenge to play, especially in the early game, since
they have a fairly strict code of conduct to follow.  Specifically,
knights are penalized for attacking sleeping or fleeing monsters
(unless the monster has stolen from the knight, or is likely to steal
the invocation artifacts, but you won't see any of these until the
quest nemesis, probably).  Annoyingly enough, a monster can start to
flee, turn around, attack you, and still qualify as fleeing, which
makes it difficult to not get the "You caitiff!" message.  If you see
this message much during the early game, be wary of #praying, as your
alignment may well be negative, leading to bad results.  Later in the
game, your alignment will probably be high enough that you don't need
to worry too much about it.  Knights can also #ride their starting
pony; if you wield a lance while riding, you have a chance of jousting,
which does extra damage but can break your lance; I'd recommend waiting
for a while before attempting this.  You can also apply the lance to
hit squares that aren't immediately next to you, which is almost as
good as throwing weapons.  Do it.  Finally, knights have the ability to
#jump like chess knights (so, two squares horizontally and one
vertically, or vice-versa), which can be useful for getting out of
trouble.  Or crossing watery levels.

The quest is populated largely by quasits, imps, and jellies.  Make
sure you have sleep resistance before you try to enter the castle.  The
quest levels are swamp; be careful about falling into the water
(consider levitation, or, better yet, #ride a flying monster).  There's
a neutral temple halfway through the quest.  The quest nemesis, Ixoth,
is a fire-breathing dragon.  In addition to hitting harder than a
standard dragon, it has a magical attack.  As mentioned above, the
prohibition against attacking sleeping/fleeing monsters doesn't apply
to those which try to steal the invocation artifacts, including Ixoth.
Use that to your advantage.

The quest artifact is the Magic Mirror of Merlin.  When carried, it
confers magic resistance, telepathy, and doubles the damage of #turning
undead, force bolt, magic missile, non-skilled cone of cold, drain
life, and wands of striking.  Since knights can get skilled in attack,
healing, and clerical spells, this can be very useful.  Incidentally,
if you apply or wield the mirror, it'll tell you things--only
guaranteed to be true if the mirror is blessed--but that's probably not
overly useful.  It's also the only non-weapon quest artifact that
doesn't do anything when #invoked.  Clearly, the best benefit is the
doubled spell damage; take advantage of it!

Monks:
Monks, like knights, have restrictions on what they can and cannot do
in the course of the game, but monks have more restrictions, some of
which are almost impossible to ignore.  For one thing, monks are
supposed to be vegetarian, though the penalty for eating meat is fairly
minor; to make up for this, they gain far more intrinsics by levelling
up than any other role.  On the more extreme end, monks cannot use body
armor (shirts, cloaks, and robes are fine) without a very large
increase in the chance of missing, which means that monks will
generally have a higher armor class than the other roles.  Monks are
also not supposed to hit with wielded weapons, but there's no penalty
for doing so (though there are bonuses for fighting without a weapon,
which amounts to much the same thing).  Monks can get to Grand Master
in unarmed combat, significantly raising attack power.  They're also
good spell casters, especially with the starting robe.

The quest is mostly populated by earth elementals and xorns, both of
which will come through walls to get you; if you need to rest and heal,
you may need to go up to a cleared level.  There's an unaligned altar
at the quest home and a second one at the bottom of the quest.  The
filler levels are regular room-and-corridor, though, again, the xorns
and elementals aren't limited to coming through the corridors.  The
quest nemesis, Master Kaen, hits really hard (two attacks for 16-32
damage each, plus some nasty magic attacks (the worst spells will only
be cast when he's next to you).  He'll also pick up the quest artifact
as soon as he awakens, which makes him even worse.  You really don't
want to stand next to this guy while fighting.  A standard
recommendation is to levitate over the lava and use ranged attacks; if
levitation's not available, try walling yourself off with boulders.  Do
not underestimate Kaen.

The quest artifact, the Eyes of the Overworld, makes this pain
worthwhile.  It is a set of lenses that give magic resistance just by
being held, and give the ability to see through walls, immunity from
blindness, and immunity to Archon's radiant gazes when worn; #invoking
the Eyes gives enlightenment, so you can check your resistances, luck,
and whether or not it's safe to pray.
